        <div class="tipsbody" id="tipsBody">
        <div class="whyhead">
          <h3><span class="sp">✦</span> Why does this matter? <span class="sp">✦</span></h3>
          <p>MemoBrick turns your photo into tiny bricks. These three things help us capture
           the most detail and get the best result.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="whygrid">
        <figure class="whycard"><span class="whyimg w1" role="img" style="background-image:url({{ 'mb-3b9218a097.jpg' | asset_url }})" aria-label="Fill the frame: a close-cropped portrait works, a full-body shot across a room does not"></span></figure>
        <figure class="whycard"><span class="whyimg w2" role="img" style="background-image:url({{ 'mb-78f2097aeb.jpg' | asset_url }})" aria-label="Light on the face: front or side light works, a bright window behind does not"></span></figure>
        <figure class="whycard"><span class="whyimg w3" role="img" style="background-image:url({{ 'mb-411d9fd9a3.jpg' | asset_url }})" aria-label="Keep the background quiet: a plain wall works, a busy patterned wall does not"></span></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="guide-cols">
        <div class="tips">
          <h3 class="tips-h">Seven moves that fix almost everything</h3>
          <ol class="tips-list">
            <li><b>Crop in first.</b> Use <span class="pill">+</span> and drag the picture until the subject fills the board. Do this before anything else — every other setting depends on it.</li>
            <li><b>Can't bear to crop?</b> Hit <span class="pill">Fit whole photo</span> and choose a border brick. The margin becomes part of the design instead of a bad crop.</li>
            <li><b>Leave <span class="pill">Auto levels</span> on.</b> It rescues flat, hazy and underexposed phone photos before a single brick is chosen. Turn it off only if your photo is already graded.</li>
            <li><b>Push <span class="pill">Detail</span> to 40–60% for faces.</b> It sharpens at stud resolution, which is what keeps eyes and jawlines readable. Drop it near 0 for logos and flat graphics.</li>
            <li><b>Keep <span class="pill">Blend shades</span> around 70–85% for people.</b> It mixes neighbouring bricks into shades we don't stock. For type, logos or bold graphics, drop it to 0 for clean edges.</li>
            <li><b>Judge it with <span class="pill">Step-back view</span>.</b> Mosaics are built up close and looked at from across a room. Nose-to-screen is the wrong distance to make decisions at.</li>
            <li><b>Fix the last few studs by hand.</b> <span class="pill">Touch up bricks</span> lets you place individual bricks — an eye that's gone soft or a stray dark stud on a cheek takes about thirty seconds.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <aside class="tips-side">
